<article class="env-corollary" id="06QM">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06QM">Corollary <span data-tag="06QM">3.5.9.25</span>.</a> Let $n$ be an integer, and suppose we are given an inverse system of Kan complexes </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \cdots \rightarrow X(3) \rightarrow X(2) \rightarrow X(1) \rightarrow X(0) \]
</div>
<p> where the transition maps are Kan fibrations. If each of the transition maps $X(k+1) \rightarrow X(k)$ is $n$-truncated, then the natural map $\varprojlim X(k) \rightarrow X(0)$ is also $n$-truncated. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      By virtue of Corollary <a data-tag="056P" href="/tag/056P">3.5.9.24</a>, it will suffice to show that every lifting problem 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ \operatorname{\partial \Delta }^{m} \ar [r] \ar [d] &amp;  \varprojlim X(k) \ar [d] \\ \Delta ^{m} \ar@ {--&gt;}[ur] \ar [r] &amp;  X(0) } \]
</div>
<p>
       admits a solution for $m \geq n+2$. This can be broken into a sequence of lifting problems 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ \operatorname{\partial \Delta }^{m} \ar [r] \ar [d] &amp;  X(k+1) \ar [d] \\ \Delta ^{m} \ar@ {--&gt;}[ur] \ar [r] &amp;  X(k), } \]
</div>
<p>
       each of which admits a solution by virtue of our assumption that the transition map $X(k+1) \rightarrow X(k)$ is an $n$-truncated Kan fibration (Corollary <a data-tag="056P" href="/tag/056P">3.5.9.24</a>). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</p></p></p></p></article>
